#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360823 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02991364-b415-5593-9a79-441d1bc3e524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438640} {:answer/id #uuid "8149ff14-001b-5709-8795-c16ffb823815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8149ff14-001b-5709-8795-c16ffb823815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438641} :answer-field/id #uuid "67616cec-be6c-5e26-881c-d38eeb8cbc05" :answer-field/key "At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439349} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27aca29d-adac-5da5-979e-bf019c9f7e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438642} {:answer/id #uuid "72677ee7-465d-51d2-8a9e-8bf69e29a86e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72677ee7-465d-51d2-8a9e-8bf69e29a86e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438643} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d62ff8d-95ed-5dd5-9434-def79d563c0e" :answer-field/key "At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439350} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d1129e6-cd92-58d0-8181-e4616216f936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438644} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa956fd-de7f-5971-bd20-88593e558254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aa956fd-de7f-5971-bd20-88593e558254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438645} :answer-field/id #uuid "572d6ef2-b778-5091-9ae8-150ff55b2bb6" :answer-field/key "At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "90e895ee-1be8-55c1-ab2d-a3596627f1b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-97487" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4x{y}^{2}$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=-4x{y}^{2}.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/96/96c937cf10d45d8870a4a56e257d184dcf2e4caf11312c05df9f385f9c5ee7a8.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement “At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative” is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the first quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the first quadrant.\n\n- Statement “At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive” is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\n- Statement “At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative” is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the fourth quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360825 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b88eec4-4bca-5a0c-8880-b03758a9562c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438646} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b8d18f-d196-594c-8c33-0a3b063ff4fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0b8d18f-d196-594c-8c33-0a3b063ff4fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438647} :answer-field/id #uuid "a57f0197-ee41-5615-824b-b099007a4ea7" :answer-field/key "At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>-2$ and $y>-2,$ the slope of the slope field is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439352} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09a982b0-26c1-598f-bb2a-081c92298f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438648} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e21d2c-a5eb-5d88-b6f1-1f1bb6ae21fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09a982b0-26c1-598f-bb2a-081c92298f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438648} :answer-field/id #uuid "30a269a3-6717-5dbd-aa51-fbb9a794d6c2" :answer-field/key "At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<-2$ and $y>-2,$ the slope of the slope field is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439353} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccb719f3-4ad9-549b-8f43-db9fde533258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438650} {:answer/id #uuid "b9393b52-2c4f-58b9-9b1c-b3c269d74cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9393b52-2c4f-58b9-9b1c-b3c269d74cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438651} :answer-field/id #uuid "e284995b-eb87-5cca-a13d-12a99f42075a" :answer-field/key "At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<-2$ and $y<-2,$ the slope of the slope field is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a885d23a-7465-5389-b01f-26298ac26e61" :question/math-academy-id "q-97495" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(x+2)(y+2)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=(x+2)(y+2).$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=-2$ or $y=-2.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/7d/7d225d82eee3043380eda76506b28e81aee06d42a232348b91b0d5ceb04f3318.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/bd/bd9eedd3b8d82415e8f3126236f2e3a3b4e326a7072057bbd21406da92a08139.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement “At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>-2$ and $y>-2,$ the slope of the slope field is positive” is true. If $x>-2$ and $y>-2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement “At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<-2$ and $y>-2,$ the slope of the slope field is positive” is false. If $x<-2$ and $y>-2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement “At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<-2$ and $y<-2,$ the slope of the slope field is positive” is true. If $x<-2$ and $y<-2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362211 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4842db83-277f-5ac6-9756-7d71ed57a360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438652} {:answer/id #uuid "aff73e0e-7657-51b7-a7f5-1be162f86243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4842db83-277f-5ac6-9756-7d71ed57a360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438652} :answer-field/id #uuid "3021006b-6d7f-5efe-b7a1-726f767d922d" :answer-field/key "At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<1$ and $y<1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439355} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a32b2ab2-8963-54b9-9a32-d18a9a92c5fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438654} {:answer/id #uuid "9b16c32e-6930-5573-ab6e-d3adf9fffe07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a32b2ab2-8963-54b9-9a32-d18a9a92c5fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438654} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9933f56-9026-5d7c-b64d-f7f6c2a23d9e" :answer-field/key "At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>1$ and $y<1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439356} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6c52061-cde6-58fd-9539-45c706276407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438656} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf6408d-db98-52dd-9aa1-9d5f8b43eba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcf6408d-db98-52dd-9aa1-9d5f8b43eba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438657} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab818855-7afa-5726-ad99-5b0158d162ae" :answer-field/key "At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>1$ and $y>1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439357} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02f02c28-218d-5002-8dec-284e88dcbc35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668745} {:answer/id #uuid "be2fad4c-0d0d-52a9-bda4-d5157c58f8dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02f02c28-218d-5002-8dec-284e88dcbc35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668745} :answer-field/id #uuid "24e2accc-de79-5b59-9349-8d3ce4e4465d" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3ca0f65d-7276-53ff-adf8-200ee3f85dcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-97534" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(x-1)(y-1)^{2}$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=(x-1)(y-1)^{2}.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=1$ or $y=1.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/07/075264fa042adc64e160a83f03a2b125dd5a9b0927fc3ed318778c0004689ec4.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/57/5716d59a745fc148b504a4db523286d7c7209fe793fcf369a90d220125f7e9fb.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement “At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<1$ and $y<1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive” is false. If $x<1$ and $y<1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement “At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>1$ and $y<1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative” is false. If $x>1$ and $y<1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement “At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>1$ and $y>1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive” is true. If $x>1$ and $y>1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24b46e4f-6125-5427-8033-f6b5e164dc44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2c/2c71db516bb0ab1514fe6e8979c980210c396fcafb88fd566ae9754ad0792bc8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360993} {:answer/id #uuid "4fec0a30-ce6c-5251-9629-d6a4cc45a554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4e/4ea9eceec3de9b414efa87954d869119caf0748f152186aff5ede9bbade3f1fc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361187} {:answer/id #uuid "7702d12c-f585-5e66-b67b-93c532d96223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/74/7428d46543d8dd2def3e48b980643572dd661f5f7edc6cba304d64eb99439403.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361365} {:answer/id #uuid "fbcaa236-6e83-556d-9413-da91fcda30a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cb/cb58d805bed05b6846fd0ab7657f8e05c65eb69562a6cda494689e44745fb9d1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361962} {:answer/id #uuid "fea37d7a-163c-5da3-8f1d-0d6bab2ddb5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d9/d94451671073d38af905b1190a15f97e6419a3a6052f83e53caa5a94b4a7000a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fec0a30-ce6c-5251-9629-d6a4cc45a554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4e/4ea9eceec3de9b414efa87954d869119caf0748f152186aff5ede9bbade3f1fc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361187} :answer-field/id #uuid "baa9da19-6193-5649-bda4-a423b8957b5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b2eadfcb-db6a-5800-9cd9-5adcd903a84a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98229" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)$ varies with both $x$ and $y,$ which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope field varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, we note the following slope field does not vary with $y$ given a fixed value of $x.$\n\n![](images/4e/4ea9eceec3de9b414efa87954d869119caf0748f152186aff5ede9bbade3f1fc.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line, the slopes do not vary.\n\n![](images/fe/fe2169790c1ee1d823937235bedd20ff10ecca662f030fd03dbfe23434517e47.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f989087-946d-5b3c-a851-11e0aad7c255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4e/4e286da41a3a0026ff7ee55649be2f0bdf5209e407a0f6fe96b3bbffa1295fb5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360891} {:answer/id #uuid "1110b134-e3de-5a68-abe0-02cce56908e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0b/0be25701c9d678977655081af50779401b207cbcd91a4c04cb32e8740180ae45.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360897} {:answer/id #uuid "604167fb-e12a-5163-b44a-4e20940c69b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b4/b433458640a60ab79880fe94549be1071420948c1c809d5c43576d4aeed45510.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361270} {:answer/id #uuid "688ee605-f5bc-54c3-8640-8f71633d1d5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8b5ff8f702e76cb7108ae458447b38eed4d18e38336f54279f25d1c59c02e3b4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361307} {:answer/id #uuid "d569e669-62ba-5faa-80b1-48870007be96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ae/ae2c8de966219eb473c42fa809bfde49b52c7c88efaf83fc7ab0d602d734e722.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d569e669-62ba-5faa-80b1-48870007be96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ae/ae2c8de966219eb473c42fa809bfde49b52c7c88efaf83fc7ab0d602d734e722.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361791} :answer-field/id #uuid "5714239c-fc7c-5b2a-8e73-725bd3d3ae1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f091f74e-0c30-5dbb-bb79-6cf50cc68036" :question/math-academy-id "q-98233" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)$ varies with both $x$ and $y,$ which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope field varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, we note the following slope field does not vary with $x$ given a fixed value of $y.$\n\n![](images/ae/ae2c8de966219eb473c42fa809bfde49b52c7c88efaf83fc7ab0d602d734e722.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a horizontal line, the slopes do not vary.\n\n![](images/e2/e243f3fb7515d9858599775ce0391f85f1de237f08380e1ec3b7403b1ecdbf53.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363771 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8a910c4-0f01-5ca2-bfbc-c7022a30631b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "gt+2.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8a910c4-0f01-5ca2-bfbc-c7022a30631b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "gt+2.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363773} :answer-field/id #uuid "da85f45d-b90b-583a-add9-dabfbfacbc6d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4c77db7c-70e0-5c92-8742-5d6e2b2a3afc" :question/math-academy-id "q-334967" :question/problem "A research buoy of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ is released from a platform and falls vertically downward with an initial velocity of $2.8\\,\\text{m/s}.$ Let $v(t)$ be the buoy's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction. The air resistance acting on the buoy is negligible, and the only other force acting on it is its weight due to gravity.\n\nFind the equation for $v(t).$ Express your answer in terms of time $t$ and acceleration due to gravity $g.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nSince air resistance is negligible, the only force acting on the buoy is its weight due to gravity. The weight $w$ of the buoy is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =w \\\\  & =mg \\\\  & =4g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$  Substituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the buoy, we get\n\n$$\n4g=4\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}=g.\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate to solve for $v(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{dv}{dt}\\,dt=g\\int dt\n$$\n\n$$\nv(t)=gt+C\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.  We're told that the initial velocity of the buoy is $2.8\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $v(0)=2.8$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\nv(0)=g\\cdot 0+C\n$$\n\n$$\n2.8=0+C\n$$\n\n$$\nC=2.8\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the buoy $t$ seconds after release is\n\n$$\nv(t)=gt+2.8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363774 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba2cdd57-1593-5ed8-b5c2-9d7ff009dfb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "gt+4.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba2cdd57-1593-5ed8-b5c2-9d7ff009dfb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "gt+4.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363776} :answer-field/id #uuid "be1a5c55-c367-5f74-9804-d3aaf58d0bf2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6a149e59-54d5-54ba-b3ae-62ba012b606a" :question/math-academy-id "q-335056" :question/problem "A sealed medical courier capsule of mass $9\\,\\text{kg}$ is released from rest with an initial downward velocity of $4.6\\,\\text{m/s}.$ Let $v(t)$ be the capsule's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction. The air resistance acting on the capsule is negligible, and the only other force acting on it is its weight due to gravity.\n\nFind the equation for $v(t).$ Express your answer in terms of time $t$ and acceleration due to gravity $g.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nSince air resistance is negligible, the only force acting on the capsule is its weight due to gravity. The weight $w$ of the capsule is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =w \\\\  & =mg \\\\  & =9g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$  Substituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the capsule, we get\n\n$$\n9g=9\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}=g.\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate to solve for $v(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{dv}{dt}\\,dt=g\\int dt\n$$\n\n$$\nv(t)=gt+C\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.  We're told that the initial velocity of the capsule is $4.6\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $v(0)=4.6$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\nv(0)=g\\cdot 0+C\n$$\n\n$$\n4.6=0+C\n$$\n\n$$\nC=4.6\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the capsule $t$ seconds after release is\n\n$$\nv(t)=gt+4.6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363777 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11005bbc-8af7-583e-aec7-7758bdf39230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11005bbc-8af7-583e-aec7-7758bdf39230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363779} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ebea1a1-68bb-502f-9092-ef33e74a735f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "332ae79c-453f-54f9-bfb4-03941fcbdcb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-335064" :question/problem "A compact geological sensor of mass $6\\,\\text{kg}$ is launched vertically downward with an initial velocity of $1.8\\,\\text{m/s}.$ Let $v(t)$ be the sensor's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance acting on the sensor is negligible, and the only other force acting on it is its weight due to gravity.\n\nWhat is the velocity of the sensor after $3$ seconds? Assume that acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nThe velocity after $3$ seconds is\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nSince air resistance is negligible, the only force acting on the sensor is its weight due to gravity. The weight $w$ of the sensor is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =w \\\\  & =mg \\\\  & =6g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$  Substituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the sensor, we get\n\n$$\n6g=6\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}=g.\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate to solve for $v(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{dv}{dt}\\,dt=g\\int dt\n$$\n\n$$\nv(t)=gt+C\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration. We're told that the initial velocity of the sensor is $1.8\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $v(0)=1.8$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\nv(0)=g\\cdot 0+C\n$$\n\n$$\n1.8=0+C\n$$\n\n$$\nC=1.8\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the sensor $t$ seconds after release is\n\n$$\nv(t)=gt+1.8.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity after $3$ seconds is\n\n$$\nv(3)=9.8\\cdot 3+1.8=31.2\\,\\text{m/s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12524418-54f5-5694-a948-79f9ee9e75ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12524418-54f5-5694-a948-79f9ee9e75ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363782} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a54b652-07e9-513d-b4ad-f67313bdcc74" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "319b53e6-31a2-50cd-ba68-0bf430c968e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-335105" :question/problem "A reconnaissance supply canister of mass $7\\,\\text{kg}$ is launched vertically downward with an initial velocity of $3.1\\,\\text{m/s}.$ Let $v(t)$ be the canister's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance acting on the canister is negligible, and the only other force acting on it is its weight due to gravity.\n\nWhat is the velocity of the canister after $5$ seconds? Assume that acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nThe velocity after $5$ seconds is\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nSince air resistance is negligible, the only force acting on the canister is its weight due to gravity. The weight $w$ of the canister is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =w \\\\  & =mg \\\\  & =7g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$  Substituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the canister, we get\n\n$$\n7g=7\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}=g.\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate to solve for $v(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{dv}{dt}\\,dt=g\\int dt\n$$\n\n$$\nv(t)=gt+C\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.  We're told that the initial velocity of the canister is $3.1\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $v(0)=3.1$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\nv(0)=g\\cdot 0+C\n$$\n\n$$\n3.1=0+C\n$$\n\n$$\nC=3.1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the canister $t$ seconds after release is\n\n$$\nv(t)=gt+3.1.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity after $5$ seconds is\n\n$$\nv(5)=9.8\\cdot 5+3.1=52.1\\,\\text{m/s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "447d9f62-9d9c-505b-8462-1ec34485d578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}-\\frac{k}{30}=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363785} {:answer/id #uuid "9e5f7d7a-7cbe-5fec-93d7-9d7f67ce3ef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{30}{k}v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363786} {:answer/id #uuid "5d5ff10f-e3df-5d2f-afcb-e4b00fd77106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{k}{30}v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363787} {:answer/id #uuid "f4695519-9f64-57a2-bd27-8719945553a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{k}{30}=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363788} {:answer/id #uuid "d9effbcb-d79c-5a98-9fd4-5991992e6d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}-\\frac{k}{30}v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d5ff10f-e3df-5d2f-afcb-e4b00fd77106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{k}{30}v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363787} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d61b3c6-2d6e-50da-a73b-ad5936b17de9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d40df24f-7f29-5d88-8be4-40799eaa2f95" :question/math-academy-id "q-335140" :question/problem "A supply pod of mass $30\\,\\text{kg}$ free-falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the pod's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after the descent began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the pod is proportional to its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k>0$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nWhich differential equation best models the velocity? Assume that the acceleration due to gravity is $g,$ and the only forces acting on the pod are the weight due to gravity and drag." :question/worked-solution "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nIn our case, there are two forces acting on the pod:\n\n- The force due to gravity, given by the weight $w$ of the pod, which equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =mg \\\\  & =30g\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The force due to drag (air resistance) $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity of the pod (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD=-kv,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Note that, since the drag, in $N=\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ is the product of the constant $k$ and velocity, in $\\text{m/s},$ the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{N}}{\\text{m/s}}=\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2}}{\\text{m/s}}=\\text{kg/s}.\n$$\n\nSumming the forces, the resultant force acting on the pod is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=30g-kv.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the pod, we have\n\n$$\n30g-kv=30\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, simplifying and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}+kv & =30g \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{k}{30}v & =g.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "136e28ce-2613-5bbf-934c-031695014b60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}-\\frac{1}{4}v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363792} {:answer/id #uuid "4917cdea-2bc4-52c6-b6ae-2c19cbb2dbde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}-4v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363793} {:answer/id #uuid "cf553b48-b81b-528f-aff9-39a29c9dcbf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}-\\frac{1}{4}=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363794} {:answer/id #uuid "bbcebaaf-2064-5cd6-bc14-897c0220e71b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{1}{4}v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363795} {:answer/id #uuid "d5a9ae9b-4cfc-501d-8d66-9c12a92563a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+4v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbcebaaf-2064-5cd6-bc14-897c0220e71b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{1}{4}v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363795} :answer-field/id #uuid "a019b1e8-9658-5f5f-9b98-0f665e25075d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2a9a8b8e-c8b7-57f0-ac19-df9c40d833df" :question/math-academy-id "q-335147" :question/problem "A storage module of mass $60\\,\\text{kg}$ is projected vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the module's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the module is proportional to their velocity with a constant of proportionality $k=15\\,\\text{kg/s}$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nWhich differential equation best models the velocity? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g$, and the only forces acting on the module are the weight due to gravity and drag." :question/worked-solution "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nIn our case, there are two forces acting on the storage module:\n\n- The force due to gravity, given by the weight $w$ of the module, which equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =mg \\\\  & =60g\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The force due to drag (air resistance) $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity of the module (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD=-kv=-15v\n$$\n\nSumming the forces, the resultant force acting on the module is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=60g-15v.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the module, we have\n\n$$\n60g-15v=60\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, simplifying and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}+15v & =60g \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{1}{4}v & =g.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363797 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5b4c538-9c68-5684-a61e-8c8a2c92c100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{2}{5}v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363799} {:answer/id #uuid "c928ca3f-a156-5c43-9aaa-41c99efcbef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}-\\frac{2}{5}=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363800} {:answer/id #uuid "29a1c91c-fef5-5b6c-8426-f085f5feaab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}-\\frac{5}{2}v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363801} {:answer/id #uuid "2fdf074b-ccb6-5fe3-bffe-516ad6f6f24f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{5}{2}v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363802} {:answer/id #uuid "82d0aeb0-53a3-57f2-8a11-85ac2c3be047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}-\\frac{2}{5}v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5b4c538-9c68-5684-a61e-8c8a2c92c100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{2}{5}v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363799} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c5e4209-64cc-53a5-b038-5672f0be2d75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bd9ee727-424f-50a8-bf00-3b9abd9fc26d" :question/math-academy-id "q-335157" :question/problem "A cargo crate of mass $55\\,\\text{kg}$ is projected vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the crate's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the crate is proportional to their velocity with a constant of proportionality $k=22\\,\\text{kg/s}$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nWhich differential equation best models the velocity? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g$, and the only forces acting on the crate are the weight due to gravity and drag." :question/worked-solution "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nIn our case, there are two forces acting on the test dummy:\n\n- The force due to gravity, given by the weight $w$ of the dummy, which equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =mg \\\\  & =55g\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The force due to drag (air resistance) $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity of the dummy (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD=-kv=-22v\n$$\n\nSumming the forces, the resultant force acting on the dummy is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=55g-22v.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the dummy, we have\n\n$$\n55g-22v=55\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, simplifying and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}55\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}+22v & =55g \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{2}{5}v & =g.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f15a7528-ec8f-5313-8a0a-567aaeddc736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(196/3)(1-{e}^{-3t/20})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f15a7528-ec8f-5313-8a0a-567aaeddc736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(196/3)(1-{e}^{-3t/20})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363806} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9662023-9fa6-55a9-9df5-d989be3fece1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "10841404-7237-599d-a213-842eefc7c119" :question/math-academy-id "q-335184" :question/problem "A research module of mass $20\\,\\text{kg}$ is released from rest and falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the module's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the module is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the module’s velocity is $10\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $8.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the module are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t),$ expressing your answer in terms of $t$ **only.**\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that only two forces act on the body: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=20\\cdot 9.8=196\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-kv,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=196-kv.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 196-kv & =20\\,\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{k}{20}v & =9.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the body’s velocity is $10\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $8.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting these values into the ODE, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n8.3+\\frac{k}{20}\\cdot 10=9.8\\,⟹\\,k=3\\,\\text{kg/s}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{3}{20}v=9.8\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${v}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{v}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{3}{20}{v}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{v}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-3t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{v}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${v}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{v}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{3}{20}\\alpha =9.8\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{196}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${v}_{p}(t)=\\frac{196}{3}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nv(t)={v}_{c}(t)+{v}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-3t/20}+\\frac{196}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the body is released from rest. So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0)=0,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =A{e}^{-3\\cdot 0/20}+\\frac{196}{3} \\\\ 0 & =A+\\frac{196}{3} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{196}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =-\\frac{196}{3}{e}^{-3t/20}+\\frac{196}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{196}{3}(1-{e}^{-3t/20})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363807 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cc39e7b-5dc1-5f65-a137-c7025944054e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{372}{5}{e}^{-t/8}+\\frac{392}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cc39e7b-5dc1-5f65-a137-c7025944054e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{372}{5}{e}^{-t/8}+\\frac{392}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363809} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f1f3e82-61d2-5724-8971-476b6fc1bcf8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f46fe468-81f7-5f62-aa3d-6fceecb30911" :question/math-academy-id "q-335198" :question/problem "A communications beacon of mass $24\\,\\text{kg}$ is launched downward with an initial velocity of $4\\,\\text{m/s}$ and continues to fall vertically. Let $v(t)$ be the beacon's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after launch, with downward taken as the positive direction.\n\nThe air resistance on the beacon is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the beacon’s velocity is $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $8.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the beacon are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t),$ expressing your answer in terms of $t$ **only.**\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that only two forces act on the body: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=24\\cdot 9.8=235.2\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-kv,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=235.2-kv.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 235.2-kv & =24\\,\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{k}{24}v & =9.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the body’s velocity is $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $8.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting these values into the ODE, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n8.8+\\frac{k}{24}\\cdot 8=9.8\\,⟹\\,k=3\\,\\text{kg/s}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{1}{8}v=9.8\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${v}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{v}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{8}{v}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{v}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/8},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{v}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${v}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{v}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{8}\\alpha =9.8\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{392}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${v}_{p}(t)=\\frac{392}{5}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nv(t)={v}_{c}(t)+{v}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/8}+\\frac{392}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the body is launched downward with an initial velocity of $4\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0)=4,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =A{e}^{-0/8}+\\frac{392}{5} \\\\ 4 & =A+\\frac{392}{5} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{372}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =-\\frac{372}{5}{e}^{-t/8}+\\frac{392}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363810 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "640c2a08-c5b9-594d-a4ee-b810043fe1bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{132}{5}{e}^{-t/3}+\\frac{147}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "640c2a08-c5b9-594d-a4ee-b810043fe1bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{132}{5}{e}^{-t/3}+\\frac{147}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363812} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bef195a-ba36-5795-b51b-9ae68eb81865" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a67e4719-a7db-5cfd-b09c-7eb77c778f2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-335199" :question/problem "A sampling canister of mass $18\\,\\text{kg}$ is launched downward with an initial velocity of $3\\,\\text{m/s}$ and continues to fall vertically. Let $v(t)$ be the canister's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after launch, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the canister is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the canister’s velocity is $9\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $6.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the canister are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t),$ expressing your answer in terms of $t$ **only.**\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that only two forces act on the body: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=18\\cdot 9.8=176.4\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-kv,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=176.4-kv.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 176.4-kv & =18\\,\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{k}{18}v & =9.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the body’s velocity is $9\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $6.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting these values into the ODE, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n6.8+\\frac{k}{18}\\cdot 9=9.8\\,⟹\\,k=6\\,\\text{kg/s}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{1}{3}v=9.8\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${v}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{v}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{3}{v}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{v}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/3},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{v}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${v}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{v}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{3}\\alpha =9.8\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{147}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${v}_{p}(t)=\\frac{147}{5}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nv(t)={v}_{c}(t)+{v}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/3}+\\frac{147}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the body is launched downward with an initial velocity of $3\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0)=3,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =A{e}^{-0/3}+\\frac{147}{5} \\\\ 3 & =A+\\frac{147}{5} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{132}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =-\\frac{132}{5}{e}^{-t/3}+\\frac{147}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363813 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8b35a95-5201-51c7-b530-c707d78375cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8b35a95-5201-51c7-b530-c707d78375cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363815} :answer-field/id #uuid "29c36c89-335b-5c20-95c1-897f9ae79cca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "db98bbe3-1f13-548e-9c0b-85a3cb742da8" :question/math-academy-id "q-335220" :question/problem "A cargo sensor of mass $40\\,\\text{kg}$ is released from rest and falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the sensor's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction. The air resistance on the sensor is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion. At a particular moment during the fall, the sensor’s velocity is $10\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $8.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nFind, rounded to one decimal place, the velocity of the cargo sensor after $5$ seconds. Assume that only gravity, $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and drag act on the sensor.\n\nThe velocity of the cargo sensor after $5$ seconds is\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{m/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that only two forces act on the sensor: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=40\\cdot 9.8=392\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-kv,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the sensor is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=392-kv.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 392-kv & =40\\,\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{k}{40}v & =9.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the sensor’s velocity is $10\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $8.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting these values into the ODE, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n8.3+\\frac{k}{40}\\cdot 10=9.8\\,⟹\\,k=6\\,\\text{kg/s}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{3}{20}v=9.8\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${v}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{v}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{3}{20}{v}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{v}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-3t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{v}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${v}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{v}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{3}{20}\\alpha =9.8\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{196}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${v}_{p}(t)=\\frac{196}{3}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nv(t)={v}_{c}(t)+{v}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-3t/20}+\\frac{196}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the sensor is released from rest. So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0)=0,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =A{e}^{-3(0)/20}+\\frac{196}{3} \\\\ 0 & =A+\\frac{196}{3} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{196}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t):$\n\n$$\nv(t)=-\\frac{196}{3}{e}^{-3t/20}+\\frac{196}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of the cargo sensor after $t=5$ seconds is\n\n$$\nv(5)=-\\frac{196}{3}{e}^{-15/20}+\\frac{196}{3}=34.472…≈34.5\\,\\text{m/s},\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363823 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e305c58-5dbe-5a92-b826-3ad8939d2ea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e305c58-5dbe-5a92-b826-3ad8939d2ea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363825} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2bb1c30-62db-5c3d-b372-03f2aa084e04" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "61cf5e94-a6d3-52e1-8a41-7864d403aee5" :question/math-academy-id "q-335324" :question/problem "A body of mass $50\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v$ be its velocity, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the body is proportional to its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k=10\\,\\text{kg/s}$ and acts opposite to the direction of motion.\n\nWhat is the terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}}$ of the body? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the body are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\n${v}_{\\text{term}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}$" :question/worked-solution "Terminal velocity is the constant speed a falling object approaches as the upward resistive force (drag) grows to exactly balance the downward gravitational force.\nThat is, at terminal velocity (a constant), acceleration is zero, so by $F=ma,$ the resultant force is zero.\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the body: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=50\\cdot 9.8=490\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-kv=-10v.\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=490-10v.\n$$\n\nWhen the body reaches terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}},$ the resultant force is zero:\n\n$$\n490-10{v}_{\\text{term}}=0\\,⟹\\,{v}_{\\text{term}}=\\frac{490}{10}=49\n$$\n\nTherefore, the terminal velocity of the body is ${v}_{\\text{term}}=49\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363833 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74eb6124-52ec-59e5-acfe-eb63a05b7f8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74eb6124-52ec-59e5-acfe-eb63a05b7f8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363835} :answer-field/id #uuid "c503a542-4769-578f-8a2e-e84bf887bc52" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "defee367-190b-5c38-a47e-ac69223e9459" :question/math-academy-id "q-335332" :question/problem "A body of mass $12\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v$ be its velocity, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the body is proportional to its velocity and acts in the opposite direction to the motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the body’s velocity is $10\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $6.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nWhat is the terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}}$ of the body? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the body are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\n${v}_{\\text{term}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}$" :question/worked-solution "Terminal velocity is the constant speed a falling object approaches as the upward resistive force (drag) grows to exactly balance the downward gravitational force.\nThat is, at terminal velocity (a constant), acceleration is zero, so by $F=ma,$ the resultant force is zero.\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the body: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=12\\cdot 9.8=117.6\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-kv,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=117.6-kv.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 117.6-kv & =12a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the body’s velocity is $10\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $6.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}117.6-10k & =12\\cdot 6.3 \\\\ 117.6-10k & =75.6 \\\\ -10k & =-42 \\\\ k & =4.2\\,\\text{kg/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF=117.6-4.2v.\n$$\n\nWhen the body reaches terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}},$ the resultant force is zero:\n\n$$\n117.6-4.2{v}_{\\text{term}}=0\\,⟹\\,{v}_{\\text{term}}=\\frac{117.6}{4.2}=28\n$$\n\nTherefore, the terminal velocity of the body is ${v}_{\\text{term}}=28\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffa98e71-289e-5733-8304-2e1534ce0056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffa98e71-289e-5733-8304-2e1534ce0056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363838} :answer-field/id #uuid "066fd902-ac1a-5137-ae63-0a0e4b465028" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "599fa805-5ce9-549e-a9e8-aa48d09d2895" :question/math-academy-id "q-335347" :question/problem "A wooden crate of mass $14\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v$ be its velocity, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the crate is proportional to its velocity and acts in the opposite direction to the motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the crate’s velocity is $7\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $6.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nWhat is the terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}}$ of the crate? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the crate are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\n${v}_{\\text{term}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}$" :question/worked-solution "Terminal velocity is the constant speed a falling object approaches as the upward resistive force (drag) grows to exactly balance the downward gravitational force.\nThat is, at terminal velocity (a constant), acceleration is zero, so by $F=ma,$ the resultant force is zero.\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the crate: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=14\\cdot 9.8=137.2\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-kv,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the crate is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=137.2-kv.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 137.2-kv & =14a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the crate’s velocity is $7\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $6.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}137.2-7k & =14\\cdot 6.3 \\\\ 137.2-7k & =88.2 \\\\ -7k & =-49 \\\\ k & =7\\,\\text{kg/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the crate is\n\n$$\nF=137.2-7v.\n$$\n\nWhen the crate reaches terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}},$ the resultant force is zero:\n\n$$\n137.2-7{v}_{\\text{term}}=0\\,⟹\\,{v}_{\\text{term}}=\\frac{137.2}{7}=19.6\n$$\n\nTherefore, the terminal velocity of the crate is ${v}_{\\text{term}}=19.6\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4f3e257-fa08-5a9c-8fd0-ddb9b6fab0be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4f3e257-fa08-5a9c-8fd0-ddb9b6fab0be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363883} :answer-field/id #uuid "50a9ee99-0f47-5b98-bb53-cd7512ac32a7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d3e95754-af7f-5b77-a99f-13be6c803abd" :question/math-academy-id "q-335736" :question/problem "A delivery pod of mass $28\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v$ be its velocity, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the pod is proportional to its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k=7\\,\\text{kg/s}$ and acts opposite to the direction of motion.\n\nWhat is the terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}}$ of the pod? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the pod are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\n${v}_{\\text{term}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}$" :question/worked-solution "Terminal velocity is the constant speed a falling object approaches as the upward resistive force (drag) grows to exactly balance the downward gravitational force.\nThat is, at terminal velocity (a constant), acceleration is zero, so by $F=ma,$ the resultant force is zero.\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the pod: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=28\\cdot 9.8=274.4\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-kv=-7v.\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the pod is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=274.4-7v.\n$$\n\nWhen the pod reaches terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}},$ the resultant force is zero:\n\n$$\n274.4-7{v}_{\\text{term}}=0\\,⟹\\,{v}_{\\text{term}}=\\frac{274.4}{7}=39.2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the terminal velocity of the pod is ${v}_{\\text{term}}=39.2\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363884 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bfdcb0d-f7a2-5a54-aae0-374447c338aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}=12g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363886} {:answer/id #uuid "42c3f190-185b-50c4-bce2-bd978935a333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363887} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6ec5d7-a8ab-55d6-b139-dfac94967987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+12v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363888} {:answer/id #uuid "df695765-0d3d-50a2-8c5f-98132336998f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{1}{6}v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363889} {:answer/id #uuid "764f6f0e-8cd0-517f-a099-ac90d8f415a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}=\\frac{g}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42c3f190-185b-50c4-bce2-bd978935a333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363887} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ed64237-3c2e-5552-8a92-7b3b7a19b9be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7052f414-ee05-561a-a39c-520f52217183" :question/math-academy-id "q-335757" :question/problem "A snow sensor of mass $12\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the sensor’s velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after the fall began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance acting on the sensor is negligible, and the only other force acting on it is the weight due to gravity. Which differential equation best models the velocity? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nSince air resistance is negligible, the only force acting on the sensor is its weight due to gravity. The weight $w$ of the sensor is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =w \\\\  & =mg \\\\  & =12g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the sensor, we get\n\n$$\n12g=12\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}\n$$\n\nFinally, simplifying and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}=g.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377198 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66750a16-700f-5c23-a55a-30496608f331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "102.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377200} {:answer/id #uuid "67dc3a2a-113e-5c48-a4d5-b5f7e81c6779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "103.3\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377201} {:answer/id #uuid "0d67e761-f48e-5810-bebf-b6e7784e791c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "103.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377202} {:answer/id #uuid "0facc8a8-1164-510f-a7f4-a0e94be929e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "102.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377203} {:answer/id #uuid "77e0c788-e151-5687-a6d5-ff813c78241e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "103.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67dc3a2a-113e-5c48-a4d5-b5f7e81c6779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "103.3\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377201} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc7f9793-5f2d-5fa5-9828-29145c21e291" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c2fb266c-0e36-5075-b402-7e3b0f1013d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-336506" :question/problem "A coolant reservoir in a manufacturing plant can hold up to $210\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $70\\,\\text{gal}$ of coolant solution with $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved corrosion inhibitor. A more concentrated coolant solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $21\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $14\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which inhibitor enters the reservoir minus the rate at which inhibitor leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which inhibitor enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of inhibitor enters the reservoir at a rate of $21\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *inhibitor* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (21\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (21\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =10.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which inhibitor exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which inhibitor leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $70\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $21\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $14\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=70+21t-14t=7(10+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{7(10+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $14\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *inhibitor* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(14\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{7(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(14\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{7(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14Q}{7(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{10+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =10.5-\\frac{2Q}{10+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{10+t}\\,Q=10.5.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(10+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|10+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(10+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(10+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{10+t}\\,Q) & =(10+t)^{2}\\cdot 10.5 \\\\ (10+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(10+t)Q & =10.5(10+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(10+t)^{2}) & =10.5(10+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(10+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 10.5(10+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(10+t)^{2} & =10.5\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(10+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =3.5(10+t)+\\frac{C}{(10+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of inhibitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=20,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =20 \\\\ 3.5(10+0)+\\frac{C}{(10+0)^{2}} & =20 \\\\ 35+\\frac{C}{(10)^{2}} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{10}^{2}} & =-15 \\\\ C & =-15(10)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =3.5(10+t)-\\frac{15(10)^{2}}{(10+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3.5(10+t)-15{\\left(\\frac{10}{10+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $210\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=210$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =210 \\\\ 70+21{t}_{o}-14{t}_{o} & =210 \\\\ 70+7{t}_{o} & =210 \\\\ 7{t}_{o} & =140 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =20\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(20) & =3.5(10+20)-15{\\left(\\frac{10}{10+20}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =105-15{\\left(\\frac{10}{30}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈103.3\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377205 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d60df00e-8828-5ca8-b2ed-d8bd599eb07f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "93.3\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377207} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa07fe2-d9eb-58e7-adb1-02efd7fc679e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "92.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377208} {:answer/id #uuid "f8206d7a-b002-5297-86c6-ed2e4ff17c80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "93.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377209} {:answer/id #uuid "1dbf4199-9fdf-5b11-9f3c-4a9a9611b20f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377210} {:answer/id #uuid "e6800f5a-8ca4-5d4d-a314-681c9b7bdc6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8206d7a-b002-5297-86c6-ed2e4ff17c80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "93.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377209} :answer-field/id #uuid "56a930be-e052-5424-bf3a-7194b15d9c7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "47a16983-dbf1-596b-b57d-9f38439fdd0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-336513" :question/problem "A flavoring tank at a beverage factory can hold up to $150\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $75\\,\\text{gal}$ of flavoring solution with $25\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved flavoring. A more concentrated solution with concentration $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of flavoring in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of flavoring in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which flavoring enters the tank minus the rate at which flavoring leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which flavoring enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of flavoring enters the tank at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *flavoring* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =6\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which flavoring exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which flavoring leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $75\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $9\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=75+9t-6t=3(25+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of flavoring in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{3(25+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *flavoring* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(25+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(25+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6Q}{3(25+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{25+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =6-\\frac{2Q}{25+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{25+t}\\,Q=6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(25+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|25+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(25+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(25+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(25+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(25+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{25+t}\\,Q) & =(25+t)^{2}\\cdot 6 \\\\ (25+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(25+t)Q & =6(25+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(25+t)^{2}) & =6(25+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(25+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 6(25+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(25+t)^{2} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(25+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =2(25+t)+\\frac{C}{(25+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $25\\,\\text{lb}$ of flavoring. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=25,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =25 \\\\ 2(25+0)+\\frac{C}{(25+0)^{2}} & =25 \\\\ 50+\\frac{C}{(25)^{2}} & =25 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{25}^{2}} & =-25 \\\\ C & =-25(25)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =2(25+t)-\\frac{25(25)^{2}}{(25+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =2(25+t)-25{\\left(\\frac{25}{25+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $150\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=150$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =150 \\\\ 75+9{t}_{o}-6{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 75+3{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 3{t}_{o} & =75 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =25\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of flavoring in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(25) & =2(25+25)-25{\\left(\\frac{25}{25+25}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =100-25{\\left(\\frac{25}{50}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈93.8\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377212 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91e3931a-6dfb-5fbc-8b0f-dbd997b8f656" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "158.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377214} {:answer/id #uuid "0562dc6c-24dc-5a4f-a3f1-00a900d6d777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "157.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377215} {:answer/id #uuid "46790c34-6fdb-5081-bdf0-ae69ba6762d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "158.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377216} {:answer/id #uuid "976b22bc-fda8-59e2-abbb-17c2352700de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "157.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377217} {:answer/id #uuid "a8deab91-7c4b-52d1-bbed-ff4091b43b8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "159.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46790c34-6fdb-5081-bdf0-ae69ba6762d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "158.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377216} :answer-field/id #uuid "5df70905-5278-5516-abae-b8ae4602e0e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "729a2fd5-523f-5eb2-bf2f-20fedeba14f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-336516" :question/problem "A disinfectant reservoir in a water treatment facility can hold up to $200\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $50\\,\\text{gal}$ of disinfectant solution with $10\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved disinfectant. A more concentrated solution with concentration $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir minus the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of disinfectant enters the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *disinfectant* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =12\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $50\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $15\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $10\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=50+15t-10t=5(10+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *disinfectant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(10\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{10+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =12-\\frac{2Q}{10+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{10+t}\\,Q=12.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(10+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|10+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(10+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(10+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{10+t}\\,Q) & =(10+t)^{2}\\cdot 12 \\\\ (10+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(10+t)Q & =12(10+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(10+t)^{2}) & =12(10+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(10+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 12(10+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(10+t)^{2} & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(10+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =4(10+t)+\\frac{C}{(10+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $10\\,\\text{lb}$ of disinfectant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=10,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =10 \\\\ 4(10+0)+\\frac{C}{(10+0)^{2}} & =10 \\\\ 40+\\frac{C}{(10)^{2}} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{10}^{2}} & =-30 \\\\ C & =-30(10)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =4(10+t)-\\frac{30(10)^{2}}{(10+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =4(10+t)-30{\\left(\\frac{10}{10+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $200\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=200$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =200 \\\\ 50+15{t}_{o}-10{t}_{o} & =200 \\\\ 50+5{t}_{o} & =200 \\\\ 5{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =30\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(30) & =4(10+30)-30{\\left(\\frac{10}{10+30}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =160-30{\\left(\\frac{10}{40}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈158.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64b2a1d5-c1ff-542b-b36c-6db0efde0080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.17\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377244} {:answer/id #uuid "1e022231-3289-56f5-b77a-6fa5e2768d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.96\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377245} {:answer/id #uuid "339b8286-0b9a-5bce-8b4a-139dcdcd79c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.49\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377246} {:answer/id #uuid "3a37a27b-7d8d-5e83-886d-a22fd90988bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.02\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377247} {:answer/id #uuid "974b466b-a863-5b62-b0b7-196c75665716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.83\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "339b8286-0b9a-5bce-8b4a-139dcdcd79c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.49\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377246} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e36df41-d08a-5164-b00e-7830a62b1c53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3ea5d5e7-af5c-553a-9742-7d54f2384984" :question/math-academy-id "q-336734" :question/problem "A nutrient-mixing tank initially holds $720\\,\\text{L}$ of solution containing $3\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved supplement.\nA feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the supplement is pumped into the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of supplement in the tank $5$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of supplement in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which supplements enter the tank minus the rate at which supplements leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which supplements enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the supplement enters the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *supplement* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.36\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which supplements exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $720\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of supplement in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{720}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ *supplement* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{720}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{720}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{720}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{60}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.36-\\frac{Q}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{60}Q=0.36.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{60}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/60},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{60}\\alpha =0.36\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =21.6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=21.6.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/60}+21.6.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $3\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved supplement. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=3,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =3 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/60}+21.6 & =3 \\\\ A & =-18.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=21.6-18.6{e}^{-t/60}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of supplement in the tank $5$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(5)=21.6-18.6{e}^{-5/60}≈4.49\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a3f26d4-7fd0-5dfc-b30a-66eaf31e8b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17.34\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377251} {:answer/id #uuid "59e1559d-1a25-5f5b-86a0-b44ad480a144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17.94\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377252} {:answer/id #uuid "6884f190-c170-5c58-85a7-6a4669b950fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.04\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377253} {:answer/id #uuid "3dca446e-f8bb-5a17-9561-bc2230c8306c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.42\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377254} {:answer/id #uuid "9010ad44-b315-5215-8406-035745dd6ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19.01\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6884f190-c170-5c58-85a7-6a4669b950fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.04\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377253} :answer-field/id #uuid "753683c1-dfc0-5785-8a4b-d335ca58b088" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "01365019-dd55-56aa-aa06-e538a69722fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-336743" :question/problem "A processing tank initially holds $420\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $10\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved preservative.\nA feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the preservative is pumped into the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of preservative in the tank $15$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of preservative in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which preservative enters the tank minus the rate at which preservative leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which preservative enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the preservative enters the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *preservative* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which preservative exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which preservative leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $420\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $18\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n420+18t-12t=(420+6t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of preservative in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{420+6t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ *preservative* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{420+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{70+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.9-\\frac{2Q}{70+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{70+t}Q=0.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(70+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|70+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(70+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(70+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(70+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(70+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{70+t}Q) & =(70+t)^{2}\\cdot 0.9 \\\\ (70+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(70+t)Q & =0.9(70+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70+t)^{2}) & =0.9(70+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 0.9(70+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(70+t)^{2} & =0.9\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(70+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.3(70+t)+\\frac{C}{(70+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $10\\,\\text{kg}$ of preservative. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=10,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =10 \\\\ 0.3(70+0)+\\frac{C}{(70+0)^{2}} & =10 \\\\ 21+\\frac{C}{{70}^{2}} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{70}^{2}} & =-11 \\\\ C & =-11(70)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.3(70+t)-\\frac{11(70)^{2}}{(70+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.3(70+t)-11{\\left(\\frac{70}{70+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of preservative in the tank $15$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(15) & =0.3(70+15)-11{\\left(\\frac{70}{70+15}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =25.5-11{\\left(\\frac{70}{85}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈18.04\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377256 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf6b3af3-595e-5f2a-9b02-bcc7246f44a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=12-5{e}^{t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377258} {:answer/id #uuid "13d461e2-b966-5ab9-b642-de837c2fca9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=8-{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377259} {:answer/id #uuid "0c0d6e4d-2317-5060-be03-4689c9b28f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=10-3{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377260} {:answer/id #uuid "1ed3fa03-0898-5985-81d9-f0e37cb5e0ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=-9+16{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377261} {:answer/id #uuid "570d8df2-e03e-5a30-8e43-d41eb6518c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=9-2{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c0d6e4d-2317-5060-be03-4689c9b28f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=10-3{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377260} :answer-field/id #uuid "a573fd39-4b38-5a00-b6f4-d30c1ee02cba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "55a40904-7261-506e-bb45-5a9e0fad5627" :question/math-academy-id "q-336750" :question/problem "A coolant tank initially holds $500\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $7\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved anti-corrosion additive.\nA solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved additive is pumped into the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of additive $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of additive in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which additives enter the tank minus the rate at which additives leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the additive enters the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *additive* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $500\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of additive in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{500}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ *additive* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{25Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.5-\\frac{Q}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}Q=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{20}\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=10.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20}+10.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $7\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved additive. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=7,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =7 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20}+10 & =7 \\\\ A & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=10-3{e}^{-t/20}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377263 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19d2fe13-097f-5e6f-9c33-2c32b0e27d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=10-7{e}^{t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377265} {:answer/id #uuid "ed6b381a-a203-5376-a746-2de328b0ebe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=12-9{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377266} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd3ac0f-6dcc-58e2-95b1-2de9c477d9b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=-1+4{e}^{t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377267} {:answer/id #uuid "864fcaca-7914-585c-ab8c-d6bf235cc102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=4-{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377268} {:answer/id #uuid "c03d0083-ace6-59b4-99bc-ac215dea614c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=-9+12{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed6b381a-a203-5376-a746-2de328b0ebe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=12-9{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377266} :answer-field/id #uuid "9149b0d8-97dc-543b-a8ef-8e274f2d81bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e940f8bd-b1c9-5052-8127-7c721b39ac69" :question/math-academy-id "q-336753" :question/problem "A storage tank initially holds $400\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $3\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved disinfectant.\nA solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved disinfectant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of disinfectant $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of disinfectant in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which disinfectant enters the tank minus the rate at which disinfectant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the disinfectant enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *disinfectant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $400\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ *disinfectant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}Q=0.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{20}\\alpha =0.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=12.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20}+12.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $3\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved disinfectant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=3,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =3 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20}+12 & =3 \\\\ A & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=12-9{e}^{-t/20}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f7f8d63-3c73-54e5-a321-064e59a6e495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=9-7{e}^{t/15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377281} {:answer/id #uuid "936ab165-4ff7-5e7c-b165-a792940b2983" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=9-7{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377282} {:answer/id #uuid "933d636b-7f9a-5a8d-877e-255718054cb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=2-9{e}^{-t/15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377283} {:answer/id #uuid "e658c55e-dd94-5640-bd43-2b24b743ccb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=9-7{e}^{-t/15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377284} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f664fa-8d20-5d06-954d-374ce7cf36e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=9+7{e}^{-t/15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e658c55e-dd94-5640-bd43-2b24b743ccb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=9-7{e}^{-t/15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377284} :answer-field/id #uuid "66547b5b-bb7a-5943-a4eb-d13453f82fe1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "36b5068a-10fd-5970-abe3-44c13a56330c" :question/math-academy-id "q-339382" :question/problem "A dye vat initially holds $450\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $2\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye.\nA solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved dye is pumped into the vat at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of dye $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the vat $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of dye in the vat at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the vat minus the rate at which dye leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the vat.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the dye enters the vat at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the vat is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the vat.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the vat remains $450\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the vat, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{450}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{15}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{15}Q=0.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{15}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/15},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{15}\\alpha =0.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=9.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/15}+9.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the vat initially contains $2\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=2,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =2 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/15}+9 & =2 \\\\ A & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=9-7{e}^{-t/15}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377286 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "495231bb-9e61-56b4-8093-d154d6a40880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.32\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377288} {:answer/id #uuid "4dec0265-b5b6-5491-9040-ac9785af058c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.21\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377289} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ed57a2-b88d-543a-a90d-9bbd77f9672a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.98\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377290} {:answer/id #uuid "53545ea8-a0a8-5fac-8720-960a278e9b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.54\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377291} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbd1d53-820b-5967-911f-26f348052b31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.75\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbbd1d53-820b-5967-911f-26f348052b31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.75\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377292} :answer-field/id #uuid "06e0f293-e28a-5788-b1b2-c59142094521" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8f9ca797-1282-5c7a-958a-6e7a8b023c5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-339390" :question/problem "A brewing tank initially holds $350\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $8\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sugar.\nA feed solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the sugar is pumped into the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of sugar in the tank $4$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which additives enter the tank minus the rate at which additives leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the sugar enters the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $350\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{350}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{350}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{350}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{25Q}{350}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{14}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.5-\\frac{Q}{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}Q=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{14}\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=7.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14}+7.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $8\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=8,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =8 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/14}+7 & =8 \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=7+{e}^{-t/14}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of sugar in the tank $4$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(4)=7+{e}^{-4/14}≈7.75\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80c9af41-db22-5fdd-9f0e-114cc03d4dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377295} {:answer/id #uuid "d27a819d-3733-5724-9e0a-425c1701164a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377296} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac1a8be-c213-5433-be78-80bb938c8e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377297} {:answer/id #uuid "dba1b189-1202-5081-99fc-577118ac8811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377298} {:answer/id #uuid "b25a95ae-28ad-5afb-9758-ae8c6c582bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80c9af41-db22-5fdd-9f0e-114cc03d4dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377295} :answer-field/id #uuid "01c0a0d5-4bf3-54b2-a43d-067aa74d6301" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "456aacfd-49e4-5a1e-8363-4f5725f307ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-339394" :question/problem "A juice-processing tank initially holds $400\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $4\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sugar.\nA solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved sugar is pumped into the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ If the amount of sugar $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n\n$$\nQ(t)=\\alpha \\,(1-\\frac{t}{40})+\\beta {(1-\\frac{t}{40})}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants, what is the value of $\\alpha -\\beta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enter the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a liquid solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of sugar enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exit the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which sugar leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $400\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $20\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n400+20t-30t=(400-10t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{400-10t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{400-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{40-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{3Q}{40-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{40-t}Q=0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(40-t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|40-t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(40-t)^{-3}} \\\\  & =(40-t)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(40-t)^{-3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(40-t)^{-3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{40-t}Q) & =(40-t)^{-3}\\cdot 0.6 \\\\ (40-t)^{-3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(40-t)^{-4}Q & =0.6(40-t)^{-3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(40-t)^{-3}) & =0.6(40-t)^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(40-t)^{-3})\\,dt & =\\int 0.6(40-t)^{-3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(40-t)^{-3} & =0.6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(40-t)^{-2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.3(40-t)+C(40-t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $4\\,\\text{kg}$ of sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=4,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =4 \\\\ C(40-0)^{3}+0.3(40-0) & =4 \\\\ C(40)^{3}+12 & =4 \\\\ C & =-\\frac{8}{{40}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.3(40-t)-\\frac{8}{{40}^{3}}(40-t)^{3} \\\\  & =0.3\\cdot 40\\,\\left(\\frac{40-t}{40}\\right)-8{\\left(\\frac{40-t}{40}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =12\\,(1-\\frac{t}{40})-8{(1-\\frac{t}{40})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have $\\alpha =12,\\beta =-8,$ and\n\n$$\n\\alpha -\\beta =12-(-8)=20.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f681e08e-700d-5f11-a546-1b73d4a5d646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377302} {:answer/id #uuid "b28e0e34-3fc5-53eb-9f95-3e5de6d384b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377303} {:answer/id #uuid "4b12db81-cdb5-5c78-aff0-867122be9ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377304} {:answer/id #uuid "efe5c524-df1e-58a3-a335-25cf075363db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377305} {:answer/id #uuid "87a222e1-6c03-558b-9d4f-e7aa60b98bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efe5c524-df1e-58a3-a335-25cf075363db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377305} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c722bf6-15d0-5bfc-a832-c8c844cbaa11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "35a9dbe2-5b3d-52d0-b235-b8068a9e0f15" :question/math-academy-id "q-339396" :question/problem "A fruit syrup tank initially holds $240\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $7\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sweetener.\nA solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved sweetener is pumped into the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $32\\,\\text{L/min}.$ If the amount of sweetener $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n\n$$\nQ(t)=\\alpha \\,(1-\\frac{t}{30})+\\beta {(1-\\frac{t}{30})}^{4}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants, what is the value of $\\alpha -\\beta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sweetener enters the tank minus the rate at which sweetener leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sweetener enter the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of sweetener enters the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sweetener* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =1.2\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sweetener exit the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which sweetener leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $240\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $24\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $32\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n240+24t-32t=(240-8t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sweetener in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $32\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sweetener* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(32\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(32\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{30-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =1.2-\\frac{4Q}{30-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{30-t}Q=1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 4/(30-t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30-t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30-t)^{-4}} \\\\  & =(30-t)^{-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30-t)^{-4}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30-t)^{-4}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{30-t}Q) & =(30-t)^{-4}\\cdot 1.2 \\\\ (30-t)^{-4}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+4(30-t)^{-5}Q & =1.2(30-t)^{-4} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-4}) & =1.2(30-t)^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-4})\\,dt & =\\int 1.2(30-t)^{-4}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30-t)^{-4} & =1.2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(30-t)^{-3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.4(30-t)+C(30-t)^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $7\\,\\text{kg}$ of sweetener. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=7,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =7 \\\\ C(30-0)^{4}+0.4(30-0) & =7 \\\\ C(30)^{4}+12 & =7 \\\\ C & =-\\frac{5}{{30}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.4(30-t)-\\frac{5}{{30}^{4}}(30-t)^{4} \\\\  & =0.4\\cdot 30\\,\\left(\\frac{30-t}{30}\\right)-5{\\left(\\frac{30-t}{30}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & =12\\,(1-\\frac{t}{30})-5{(1-\\frac{t}{30})}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have $\\alpha =12,\\beta =-5,$ and\n\n$$\n\\alpha -\\beta =12-(-5)=17.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377307 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7203da68-a7dc-5fa7-a821-14e51c37d796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.21\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377309} {:answer/id #uuid "5c6e3ac6-f774-5924-a271-5096cb694bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.44\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377310} {:answer/id #uuid "a8c3b57f-b0fe-5ca4-bc6e-be09e8b780b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.97\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377311} {:answer/id #uuid "57a068be-87ec-53cf-a457-ecd6c727cbfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.92\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377312} {:answer/id #uuid "08fda585-15a3-51ba-b2f7-6c5b1fb1b63f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.88\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c6e3ac6-f774-5924-a271-5096cb694bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.44\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377310} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa11ba31-9d56-5bad-87cb-702df2d10932" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e6bee6b8-8870-5e26-916b-bf3b0601ff8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-339399" :question/problem "A coolant reservoir initially holds $400\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved corrosion inhibitor.\nA feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the inhibitor is pumped into the reservoir at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir $12$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which inhibitor enters the tank minus the rate at which inhibitor leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which inhibitor enters the reservoir.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the inhibitor enters the reservoir at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *inhibitor* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which inhibitor exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which inhibitor leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $400\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $16\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n400+16t-12t=(400+4t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{400+4t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ *inhibitor* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{400+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{100+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.8-\\frac{3Q}{100+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{100+t}Q=0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(100+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|100+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(100+t)^{3}} \\\\  & =(100+t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(100+t)^{3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(100+t)^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{100+t}Q) & =(100+t)^{3}\\cdot 0.8 \\\\ (100+t)^{3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(100+t)^{2}Q & =0.8(100+t)^{3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(100+t)^{3}) & =0.8(100+t)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(100+t)^{3})\\,dt & =\\int 0.8(100+t)^{3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(100+t)^{3} & =0.8\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(100+t)^{4}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.2(100+t)+\\frac{C}{(100+t)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of inhibitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=6,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =6 \\\\ 0.2(100+0)+\\frac{C}{(100+0)^{3}} & =6 \\\\ 20+\\frac{C}{{100}^{3}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{100}^{3}} & =-14 \\\\ C & =-14(100)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.2(100+t)-\\frac{14(100)^{3}}{(100+t)^{3}} \\\\  & =0.2(100+t)-14{\\left(\\frac{100}{100+t}\\right)}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir $12$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(12) & =0.2(100+12)-14{\\left(\\frac{100}{100+12}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =22.4-14{\\left(\\frac{25}{28}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & ≈12.44\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377314 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "099acc92-7fac-5696-b31e-f1fb2df34af2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.58\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377316} {:answer/id #uuid "b84c947f-ccfb-56fc-b617-b376d1a795a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.94\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377317} {:answer/id #uuid "595a8122-0ca3-5fa3-841a-170aadcc3604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.03\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377318} {:answer/id #uuid "d84483d6-8474-5c8e-8b9c-2b641cc22149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.67\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377319} {:answer/id #uuid "26b77d91-31cc-548b-acc6-bdc54133b6cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.12\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "595a8122-0ca3-5fa3-841a-170aadcc3604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.03\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377318} :answer-field/id #uuid "70d825b7-f752-5a4c-9bbe-c55b7b40186e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "46db6f1f-9edd-53d3-9387-f26ef7512b99" :question/math-academy-id "q-339400" :question/problem "A fermentation vat initially holds $500\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $8\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved nutrient.\nA feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the nutrient is pumped into the vat at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $5\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of nutrient in the vat $8$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of nutrient in the vat at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which nutrient enters the tank minus the rate at which nutrient leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which nutrient enters the vat.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the nutrient enters the vat at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *nutrient* enters the vat is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (10\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which nutrient exits the vat.\n\nTo find the rate at which nutrient leaves the vat, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the vat at time $t.$\n\nThe vat initially contains $500\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $10\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $5\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the vat after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n500+10t-5t=(500+5t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of nutrient in the vat, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{500+5t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $5\\,\\text{L/min},$ *nutrient* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(5\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{500+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{500+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{500+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{100+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{100+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{100+t}Q=0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 1/(100+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|100+t|} \\\\  & =100+t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=100+t$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(100+t)\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{100+t}Q) & =(100+t)\\cdot 0.6 \\\\ (100+t)\\frac{dQ}{dt}+Q & =0.6(100+t) \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(100+t)) & =0.6(100+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(100+t))\\,dt & =\\int 0.6(100+t)\\,dt \\\\ Q(100+t) & =0.6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(100+t)^{2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.3(100+t)+\\frac{C}{100+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the vat initially contains $8\\,\\text{kg}$ of nutrient. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=8,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =8 \\\\ 0.3(100+0)+\\frac{C}{100+0} & =8 \\\\ 30+\\frac{C}{100} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{C}{100} & =-22 \\\\ C & =-2200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.3(100+t)-\\frac{2200}{100+t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of nutrient in the vat $8$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(8) & =0.3(100+8)-\\frac{2200}{100+8} \\\\  & =32.4-\\frac{2200}{108} \\\\  & ≈12.03\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377695 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d118fc5-1c52-5566-be98-a9707e774937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377697} {:answer/id #uuid "30aa323e-111a-55db-ae16-f96b2c70da3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377698} {:answer/id #uuid "563f0bf5-f3be-54dc-b44e-fce9a03892d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377699} {:answer/id #uuid "a81b9d36-0a30-5151-bf36-ec51a5b12490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377700} {:answer/id #uuid "f84b3786-3b86-57a7-acce-0bc703bbfb0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "563f0bf5-f3be-54dc-b44e-fce9a03892d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377699} :answer-field/id #uuid "117ca5de-bfde-5504-8e9b-162dc1ee9296" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9d22711b-d1d6-5f06-ab4a-74275f71d77c" :question/math-academy-id "q-74920" :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1}=\\frac{1\\cdot (-3)-(-3)}{1-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1} & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{(x-1)y}{x-1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,-3)}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,-3)}y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-3.$"}]}
